I recently delved into the dynamic programming paradigm and came across two versions of the coin change problem:

Given a set of coin denominations \( C = \{c_1, c_2, \ldots\} \) where \( c_i \in W \) for all \( i \), and a target amount \( n \in W \):

1. Determine the **total number of ways** to produce the sum \( n \).
2. Determine the **number of distinct combinations** to produce the sum \( n \).

Using any number of each coin.

I understand these questions to differ in that one asks for the number of permutations of coins that result in the sum \( n \), while the other asks for the number of combinations that result in the sum \( n \).

For the first question, the recurrence relation was straightforward. By fixing a coin \( c_i \), the number of ways to achieve a sum \( x \) with this fixed coin is the same as the number of ways to achieve the sum \( x - c_i \). Hence, the recurrence relation is:

\[
\text{count}(x) = 
\begin{cases} 
0, & \text{if } x < 0 \\
1, & \text{if } x = 0 \\
\sum\limits_{c_i \in C} \text{count}(x - c_i), & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]

